In article <1991Aug1.013801.25478@fs7.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
>
>in the interest of promoting a magic-using character-type let's not
>make NetHack equal oppurtunity (in a sense).  wizards and perhaps
>priests and healers (let's call them all magic-users) should have
>advantages at using magic and disadvantages at using combat tools.
>bottom line: a magic-user and a fighter with equal stats should have
>different capabilities when it comes to casting spells and hack'n'-
>slashing.  this diversity is, i think, more desirable than the status
>quo.  the differences should be significant but surmountable ...
>
Ummm... I think you talked me into it.  I guess as long as my diligent
wizard who is now 18/100 strength can CARRY as much as an equivalent
barbarian, I won't begrudge barbie an edge in natural "to-hit" and "dam"
stats -- so long as I have an edge in spellcasting.

Dave, you hit the main point right on the head though, the differences
should be surmountable.

Certainly this would remain in the spirit of nethack - after all, priests,
samurais, archaeologists, etc. have certain abilities unique to their
class now.  And while we're on that subject, I think that a few more
such special abilities should come with classes:

  1) Cavemen should be somewhat immune to rotten (no effect) or tainted
     (merely throw up) food.

  2) Wizards should have a chance (based on level) to recognize an otherwise
     unidentified scroll.

  3) Similarly, rogues should have a chance to recognize a ring (thieves
     need to know about jewelry!)

  4) Knights should be immune to paralysis by fear (from ghosts/demons).
     After all, they're courageous and this class doesn't get much else
     in the way of breaks!

  5) This may already be true: healers should be immune from sickness
     attacks from the likes of Juiblex and Demogorgon.
